insert into public.articles (slug, title, excerpt, body_md, tags, min_level, published_at)
values
  ('when-a-song-takes-you-straight-back', 'When a Song Takes You Straight Back', 'A smell in a corridor, a song in a shop, a particular light in the afternoon, and your body is suddenly back there. What reminders are, and what to do when one lands.', 'You are in the supermarket and the song comes on, and before you have finished the thought your chest is tight and your legs have gone strange. Nothing has happened. You are standing next to the bread.

Then comes the second problem, which is being frightened of your own reaction in a public place, and after that the quiet decision to avoid that shop.

What a reminder actually is

When something frightening or overwhelming happens, your brain stores more than the story. It stores the sensory details, because the job of that system is speed rather than accuracy.

So the smell of a particular disinfectant, the sound of a certain engine, a kind of light, a tone of voice, a date on a calendar all get filed alongside danger. Later, when one of them turns up, the alarm fires first and the explaining comes afterward.

This is why the reaction arrives before the thought, and why it can feel so unreasonable. It is not a sign that you are going backward. It is an old filing system doing exactly what it was built to do.

The reaction is not the event

The useful distinction is between remembering and reliving.

A memory has a frame around it: this happened, it was then, I am here. A reminder can arrive without that frame, so the body responds as though it is happening now. Fast heart, tight stomach, the urge to get out, sometimes a strange distance from the room.

Putting the frame back is most of the work, and it is done with plain words rather than arguments. This is a reminder. It is 2026. I am in a shop. It was eight years ago.

What to do in the moment

Keep it short, physical and simple.

Breathe out longer than you breathe in, two or three times. Put your feet flat and press them into the floor. Hold something cold, or run your wrists under cold water if there is a tap nearby.

Then do something that makes the present concrete: name five things you can see, say the day of the week out loud, count the red things in the aisle. This is not distraction for its own sake; it is giving your senses evidence about where you actually are.

Move if you can. Walking to the end of the street and back does more than standing still and waiting for it to pass.

And let it pass without fighting it. Alarm peaks and comes down; what keeps it going is the second layer, the fear of the reaction itself.

Afterward, write down the trigger

This is the step almost nobody takes, and it is where the pattern becomes manageable.

A line is enough: Tuesday, supermarket, that song, chest and legs, lasted ten minutes. After a few weeks you will usually see a small, specific list rather than the vague sense that it can happen anywhere.

A known list is far less frightening than an unknown one, and it tells you what to prepare for: the anniversary week, hospital corridors, fireworks, a particular road, raised voices.

Prepare rather than avoid

Avoidance is understandable and it has a cost: every place you stop going makes your world a little smaller and teaches the alarm that it was right.

So aim for planned contact instead. Go to the shop at a quiet hour, with someone, having eaten, knowing you can leave. Then go again. Then go alone. Then go without the exit plan.

Keep each attempt short and repeat it soon. Repetition is what updates the filing system; one difficult visit followed by three weeks away does not.

Where the reminder is unavoidable, such as a date or a season, put it in the diary and build the week around it rather than being ambushed.

Take care of the conditions

How easily a reminder lands depends a great deal on your state.

Short sleep, hunger, pain, alcohol and too much caffeine all lower the threshold. So does a long stretch of stress with no let-up in it.

None of that is a moral matter; it is simply that an already alert system reacts to more things. Getting those closer to steady will not remove the reminders and it will take the edge off how often they hit.

What to tell the people around you

A short sentence saves a lot of confusion: certain things take me straight back, it passes in a few minutes, the best thing is to stay nearby and let me get my breath.

Tell them what not to do as well, if you know. Many people find questions in the moment unhelpful, and most find being talked at worse than being sat with.

You do not have to tell anyone what happened in order to ask for that.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if reminders are frequent, if the memories are intruding during the day, if your sleep is broken by them, or if you have been shaping your life around avoiding places and people.

Trauma responds well to treatment, including a long time afterward, and the treatments are specific rather than vague: there are a small number of approaches designed exactly for this and they work. Say to whoever you see that particular sounds, smells or dates set it off; that detail points them straight to the right kind of help.

And if the reminders have taken you somewhere dark, and you are not sure you want to be here, please tell a doctor or someone you trust today. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Trauma', 'Anxiety']::text[], 0, '2026-10-14T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('the-mistake-everyone-knows-about', 'The Mistake Everyone Knows About', 'You got something badly wrong and other people saw. Why a public error feels so much worse than a private one, and how to get through the weeks that follow it.', 'You sent the figures with the wrong number in them. You said the thing in the meeting. You missed the deadline that other people were waiting on, and now it has been noticed, discussed, and possibly fixed by somebody else.

It is nine in the evening and you are still going over it. Tomorrow you have to walk back in there.

Why public gets under your skin

A private mistake is a problem to solve. A public one adds an audience, and the audience is what turns it into shame.

Guilt is about the thing you did: I got that wrong, I need to fix it. Shame is about what the thing says about you: they have all seen what I am actually like. The first one can be acted on. The second just burns.

That is why the size of the error often has little to do with how bad it feels. A small mistake in front of people you want to respect you can hurt more than a large one nobody saw.

And if you have built your sense of yourself on being competent — the reliable one, the one who does not drop things — a visible error does not feel like an event. It feels like exposure.

The first day is about handling it, not about feeling better

Do the practical part early, because unfinished business feeds the rumination.

Name it plainly to whoever needs to know: I got this wrong, here is the effect, here is what I am doing about it, here is when it will be done. No long preamble, no case for the defense, no theatrical self-flagellation, which puts others in the position of comforting you.

Fix what can be fixed, and say what cannot. Then write one line about how it will not happen again, if there is a real change to make.

That conversation is almost always shorter and less terrible than the version you rehearsed, and it moves you from the thing being about your character to it being about a task.

What the replaying is doing

For a few days your mind will offer you the scene on a loop, usually at night, with commentary.

That is not processing. It is punishment, and it does not produce insight; by the fourth round you are not learning anything, you are only feeling worse. The lesson, if there is one, is usually available in ten minutes with a pen.

So take the ten minutes. What exactly went wrong, what was in my control, what will I do differently, who did I need to tell. Write it down, decide it is finished, and when the loop starts again say: that is written down, I am not doing it now.

Expect the evenings to be worse than the mornings. It gets louder when you are tired.

Get the scale right

Shame makes a terrible estimator, so check the numbers.

Other people are thinking about this far less than you imagine, not because they are kind, but because they are busy with their own week. By Friday most of them have moved on; you are the only one with a full transcript.

And look at the actual record rather than today. How many things have you got right in the last year? Would you write someone off for this if they did it? Has anyone you respect ever made a mistake like it?

Nearly everyone has a version of this, which is why people who have been around a while are usually much kinder about errors than people who have not.

Do not overcorrect

Two reactions tend to make the following months worse.

Hiding: going quiet, stopping volunteering, no longer putting your hand up. That reads as disengagement rather than conscientiousness, and it also removes the ordinary successes that would otherwise put this in proportion.

Overcompensating: double-checking everything to the point of being slow, sending apologetic emails for weeks, taking on extra to prove something. That broadcasts the mistake long after everyone else has filed it away, and it is exhausting.

The steadier move is to be visible and ordinary. Turn up, do the next piece of work well, do not refer to it again after the repair conversation.

Be careful what you build from it

A mistake teaches you something small and specific: check that file before sending, confirm the date, do not do that task at four in the afternoon.

What it does not teach you is that you are careless, unfit for the job, or found out. Those are conclusions shame draws, and they tend to stick around much longer than any actual consequence.

So keep the lesson narrow and in writing. A narrow lesson improves your work; a broad one about your character just makes you frightened of doing the work at all.

When it is more than a bad week

Please talk to a therapist or a counselor if the replaying has gone on for weeks, if you are dreading work every morning, if you have started avoiding people, or if one mistake has turned into a settled belief that you are not good enough.

Shame responds well to being said out loud to someone who does not flinch, which is a large part of why therapy helps with it. And if the low mood has brought your sleep or your appetite down with it, mention that to a doctor too; it is a common and treatable thing to come in with, and it is much easier to think clearly about one mistake once it lifts.', array['Shame', 'Perfectionism']::text[], 0, '2026-10-14T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('angry-at-someone-you-cannot-confront', 'Angry at Someone You Cannot Confront', 'The person has died, moved away, holds all the power, or would only deny it. What to do with real anger when the conversation that would settle it is not available.', 'You are furious with someone, and there is nowhere to put it. They died before you could say it. They would deny every word. They are your manager, or your landlord, or the hospital, and saying it plainly would cost you more than it is worth.

So you carry it, and it comes out sideways: in the car, at the dog, at the person who did nothing, at three in the morning.

Being stuck with it is its own problem

Anger is designed to move you toward something: a boundary, a correction, a conversation. When that route is closed, the energy does not simply switch off.

It usually turns into one of three things. Rumination, where you run the argument you will never have. Irritability with everyone nearby. Or a low, flat resentment that drains the color out of things and lasts for years.

None of those are a character flaw. They are what happens when a reasonable reaction has nowhere to go.

Decide honestly whether it is really closed

Before working around the conversation, check that it is actually unavailable.

Sometimes the barrier is real: they have died, they are dangerous, it would cost you your job or your housing, or they have shown repeatedly that they will twist anything you say.

Sometimes the barrier is fear, which is different. If the only thing stopping you is that it would be uncomfortable, that is worth looking at, possibly with help, because an unsaid sentence that could be said is a different problem from one that cannot.

And there is often a middle option. Not the full confrontation, but one line, once: when you say that about my parenting, I am not going to discuss it. Small, survivable, and sometimes enough.

Say it somewhere other than to them

The anger usually needs to be expressed, not resolved, and the person is not the only possible audience.

Write the letter you will never send. All of it, unedited, with the parts that are unfair to them left in; it is not a legal document. Many people find this does more in forty minutes than months of thinking.

Say it out loud to someone who is on your side, or to the empty room, or to a chair. Say it to your voice recorder and delete it. The point is to get the sentences out of your head and into the air, because unspoken anger stays at full volume indefinitely.

Then notice what the sentences are really about. Underneath most of it there is a plain statement — you were supposed to look after me, you took something I cannot get back, I did everything right and it did not matter — and finding that line is often the turn.

Let your body finish the thing

Anger is physical, and when the action it prepared you for never happens, the charge stays in your muscles.

So use it on purpose. Walk fast, lift something heavy, swim, dig, scrub, hit a pillow if that is your sort of thing. Push hard against a wall for ten seconds and let go.

That is not a metaphor. It is the simplest way to discharge what the conversation would have discharged, and it tends to drop the volume enough for the rest to be possible.

Accepting the situation is not forgiving the person

This is the part people resist most, and it is worth being precise about.

Accepting means: this happened, they are not going to admit it, there is no apology coming, and I am the one carrying the cost. That is a bitter set of facts and it is where you actually are.

It does not mean they were right, or that you forgive them, or that you must stay in touch, or that you have to feel calm about it. Those are separate questions and some of them have the answer no.

Fighting the facts — refusing to accept that no apology is coming — keeps you tied to the person far longer than accepting it does. The anger usually loosens not when they change but when you stop waiting for them to.

Choose where the energy goes

Anger with nowhere to point can be aimed, which is not the same as swallowing it.

Some people put it into a complaint that goes on a record, a letter to an organization, a safeguarding report, a review, a union. Even when nothing comes back, there is a real difference between silence and having said it to someone official.

Others put it into the thing the anger is about: the parent who looks after their own children differently, the person who makes sure it does not happen to the next patient. That is not a tidy ending, and it is better than carrying it unspent.

And some of it is grief rather than anger, and grief needs time and company rather than a target.

When to get help with it

Please talk to a doctor or a therapist if the anger is taking up most of your days, if it is coming out at people who do not deserve it, if you are drinking to manage it, or if it has settled into a resentment you cannot put down.

This is one of the things therapy handles well, partly because a therapist can be the audience the conversation never had, and partly because the skills for sitting with an unresolvable situation are teachable rather than a matter of character. If the anger is about something that was done to you, say so plainly to whoever you see; it points them to the right kind of help straight away.', array['Anger', 'DBT']::text[], 0, '2026-10-14T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('visiting-someone-who-does-not-know-you-anymore', 'Visiting Someone Who Does Not Know You Anymore', 'They ask who you are, or tell you a story that never happened. How to spend an hour with someone whose memory has gone, and what makes a visit easier for both of you.', 'You drive over, sit down, and she asks whether you have come about the room. Later she tells you her mother is collecting her at four. Your mother has been dead for thirty years.

You leave feeling hollow, and slightly ashamed of how much you wanted her to say your name.

What you are grieving is real

Nobody prepares you for mourning someone who is sitting in front of you drinking tea.

The person is here and the relationship has changed beyond recognition. You have lost the shared history, the advice, the being known by them, and you are not allowed any of the ordinary rituals, because to everyone else nothing has happened.

That is a genuine grief, and it tends to arrive in waves rather than once: at the first time she calls you by her sister''s name, the first time you leave and she does not notice, the first Christmas she does not know it is Christmas.

Feeling relief alongside the sadness is also normal, and so is feeling nothing on a given day. None of it makes you a bad son or daughter or friend.

Stop correcting, and follow instead

The instinct is to put reality back: Mom, your mother died in 1994, it is me, your daughter.

That almost never helps. It cannot be remembered, so it has to be absorbed as fresh bad news each time, and it leaves the person frightened and you both upset.

The alternative is to go where they are. If she is waiting for her mother, you can ask about her mother: what she was like, what she cooked, what she would say. If she thinks you are a nurse, you can be the nice person who has come to sit with her for a while.

That is not lying to someone. It is choosing their comfort over being technically correct, and it makes the difference between an hour that works and an hour that ends in tears.

Where you can, answer the feeling rather than the question. Someone asking to go home is usually saying they feel unsafe, and what settles that is not an explanation but a warm voice, a cup of tea and company.

Make the visit easier before you arrive

Go at the time of day when they are at their best, which for many people is the morning. Late afternoon is often harder.

Keep it short and regular rather than long and occasional. Twenty good minutes beats two draining hours, and nobody is keeping score; if they will not remember the length, you may as well choose the version that leaves you both in better shape.

Bring one thing and nothing else: a familiar song, a photograph, something to eat, hand cream, a plant to repot. Having something to do with your hands removes the pressure to make conversation, which is the part that goes wrong most often.

Two people is usually easier than one. Six is overwhelming.

What to do instead of talking

Conversation relies on recent memory, which is exactly what is missing. So lean on the things that do not.

Music from when they were young, which lasts far longer than almost anything else. Old photographs rather than new ones. Singing, which often survives when speech has gone. Touch, if they like it: holding a hand, hand cream, brushing hair. A short walk. Watching birds out of the window.

And let silence be fine. Sitting together quietly is a real visit; the need to fill it is yours, not theirs.

Keep your tone slow and warm and your sentences short. Tone and face carry the meaning long after the words stop landing.

On the hard days

Some visits go badly whatever you do. They can be agitated, cold, accusing, convinced you have stolen something.

That is the illness talking, and knowing it does not stop it hurting. Do not argue the accusation; agree that it is upsetting, change the subject, make a drink, step out for five minutes and come back in.

If the visit is going nowhere, it is allowed to end early. Leaving is not a failure, and most people with memory problems take their cue from your mood rather than the clock.

Then look after yourself on the way home: do not go straight into something demanding, and tell someone how it went. People in this position do an enormous amount of it silently.

Keep yourself in the picture

This can go on for years, which means it has to be arranged like something sustainable rather than a crisis.

Share the visits where there is anyone to share them with. Accept that your sibling who comes twice a year will have opinions and will not understand the hour you have just had.

Find one other person who is doing the same thing. Carer groups for families living with memory loss are unusually good, because what you most need is other people who already know and do not need it explained.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if you are not sleeping, dreading every visit, drinking more, or feeling nothing at all; those are signs of being past capacity rather than of not caring enough.

Ask too for the practical help that exists and is widely unclaimed: a carer''s assessment for you, day services, respite, memory clinic support, and advice on money and legal arrangements while they can still take part in those decisions. A therapist or a counselor is the right place for the grief itself, which is harder than almost anyone outside this situation understands, and which deserves more than being carried quietly on the drive home.', array['Caregiving', 'Communication']::text[], 0, '2026-10-14T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz),
  ('when-your-job-quietly-doubled', 'When Your Job Quietly Doubled', 'Someone left, nobody replaced them, and their work landed on you. How to tell what your job actually is now, and how to hand some of it back without a confrontation.', 'Two people left last spring. Their work did not leave with them; it arrived on your desk in pieces, one reasonable request at a time, and you were competent and willing, so it stayed.

Now you work through lunch, you take things home, and you cannot point to any one thing that is unreasonable. That is what makes this so hard to raise.

Why it happens so easily

Nobody decides to double your job. It accumulates, which is why there is never a moment where saying no would have been obvious.

Each piece arrives as a favor, a temporary cover, or a small thing. Each one, on its own, is genuinely small. And a competent person who says yes becomes the obvious person to ask next time, so capability quietly becomes the reason for the load.

Managers also lose track. From above, your work looks like one line on a plan; nobody has the list of forty things you actually do, and most of them assume the old job plus the new bits is roughly the old job.

Write down what you actually do

You cannot negotiate about a feeling, and right now you have a feeling. Get a list.

Spend half an hour writing every task you own, including the invisible ones: the reporting nobody asked for formally, the system you are the only person who understands, the training of new people, the covering of the phone, the chasing.

Put a rough weekly hour estimate on each, mark which were yours a year ago and which arrived since, and total it. Most people find a number well over their contracted hours, and seeing it written down changes the conversation from complaining to arithmetic.

This document is the single most useful thing you can make here, and almost nobody makes it.

Have the conversation about priorities, not effort

The instinct is to say I am overloaded, which invites reassurance and changes nothing.

A better opening is to bring the list and ask for decisions: here is everything I am carrying, it comes to about fifty hours, here are the three things I think matter most, which of the rest should stop, move, or wait?

That does three things. It makes the load visible, it puts the choice where the authority is, and it makes you look organized rather than reluctant.

Go in with a proposal rather than only a problem. One extra pair of hands two days a week. This piece moving to another team. That report going quarterly instead of monthly. The weekly meeting that could be an email.

And ask for the title and the money if the job has genuinely changed into a bigger one. If the answer is no, that is information worth having.

Say no to the next piece, in a way that is easy to accept

Most of the growth happens in small moments, so that is where to hold the line.

I can take that on if something else comes off, which would you rather I drop? Then wait. That question is not aggressive and it is extremely hard to answer with everything.

Or: I can do it by Thursday rather than tomorrow. Or: I can do the first part, not the whole thing. Or simply: not this week.

Say it once, warmly, and do not explain for three sentences. Over-explaining invites negotiation.

And stop the silent absorbing. Doing things nobody knows about at ten at night is kind, invisible, and the reason the arrangement looks like it works.

Make the cost visible if nothing changes

If the real answer from above is to carry on, make the situation legible rather than quietly impossible.

Put things in a queue and say so: that is in the list, I expect to get to it in two weeks. Send a short monthly note of what got done and what did not. Keep a record of what you flagged and when, which matters if this ever becomes a dispute.

Stop rescuing every deadline. Deadlines that are only met by your unpaid evenings tell everyone the staffing is fine.

This feels uncomfortable and it is far better than the alternative, which is that the whole arrangement depends on you never getting ill.

Decide what you will do if it does not improve

Give it a date. If nothing has changed by the end of the quarter, then what?

Options are usually: formally reduce what you own, apply internally for the bigger role with the bigger pay, go to your union or human resources, or start looking elsewhere. Writing down which one you would pick takes some of the trapped feeling out, even before you act.

And be honest about the part that is yours. If you find it almost impossible to let a standard slip, or to be the person who did not get it done, that is worth knowing, because a workplace will happily accept an unlimited amount of that.

When to get help

Please talk to a doctor if you are not sleeping, waking at three, getting ill repeatedly, dreading Mondays from Saturday onward, or drinking more to switch off. Those are the standard warning lights, and arriving with them early is far better than arriving once you cannot work at all.

A therapist is worth it if the inability to put work down is the real engine, or if your sense of being worth something has become tied to how much you absorb. And if the load is not actually survivable, say so to someone plainly — a doctor, a union, a professional you trust — rather than waiting for the point where the decision gets made for you.', array['Burnout', 'Assertiveness']::text[], 0, '2026-10-14T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz)
